Another observation:

> input <- SpectronauttoMSstatsFormat(MSstats.report, intensity = "PeakArea", qvalue_cutoff = 0.01)

** Intensities with great than 0.01 in EG.Qvalue are replaced with zero.
** All peptides are unique peptides in proteins.
** No multiple measurements in a feature and a run.

> head(MSstats.report)
  R.Condition          R.FileName R.Replicate                               PG.ProteinAccessions
1     control 20150224_2_SWATH_C1           1 A0A023T672;A0A0G2JEA9;A0A0G2JFX7;A0A0N4SUH6;Q9CWZ3
2     control 20150224_2_SWATH_C1           1 A0A023T672;A0A0G2JEA9;A0A0G2JFX7;A0A0N4SUH6;Q9CWZ3
3     control 20150224_2_SWATH_C1           1 A0A023T672;A0A0G2JEA9;A0A0G2JFX7;A0A0N4SUH6;Q9CWZ3
4     control 20150224_2_SWATH_C1           1 A0A023T672;A0A0G2JEA9;A0A0G2JFX7;A0A0N4SUH6;Q9CWZ3
5     control 20150224_2_SWATH_C1           1 A0A023T672;A0A0G2JEA9;A0A0G2JFX7;A0A0N4SUH6;Q9CWZ3
6     control 20150224_2_SWATH_C1           1                    A0A023T778;G3UZW7;P61327;Q9CQL1

The function reports all peptides to be unique in proteins...well, looking at the PG.ProteinAccessions column I would argue: Yes, technically they are, because Spectronaut reports a ;-separated  list of protein ACCs in this field. So the peptides are only unique after protein grouping. Is that really what you want? This would accordingly give you protein group inference in the end, not a protein inference.

Greetings,
Tobi

